<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="chp-scripts-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Scripts &amp; Functions</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			In the previous chapters, you learned how to store values in <xref ref="sec-variables" text="custom">variables</xref> and
			experiment with expressions directly in the Command Window.
			Until now, most of your code has been entered directly in the Command Window — one line at a time, discarded after each session.
			This chapter introduces two tools that let you save and reuse your work: <term>scripts</term> and <term>functions</term>.
		</p>

		<p>
			A script is a saved file of MATLAB commands that you can run repeatedly.
			Think of it as a recipe: you write the steps once, and MATLAB follows them every time you run the file.
			Scripts are ideal when you want to repeat the same sequence of calculations with different input values.
		</p>

		<p>
			A function goes one step further: it accepts <term>inputs</term>, performs a calculation, and returns <term>outputs</term>.
			Functions let you package a useful computation into a reusable tool, just like MATLAB's built-in commands
			<pf>sqrt</pf>, <pf>abs</pf>, and <pf>floor</pf>.
			This chapter covers two major concepts:
			<dl width="narrow">
				<li><title>Scripts</title>
					<p>
						A sequence of MATLAB commands saved in a <pf>.m</pf> file.
						Scripts share the base workspace, run top to bottom, and are ideal for
						one-time tasks or structured computations with no inputs or outputs.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li><title>Functions</title>
					<p>
						A named, reusable block of code that accepts inputs and returns outputs.
						Functions use their own private workspace, keeping internal variables
						separate from the Command Window.
					</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
		</p>

		<p>
			By the end of this chapter, you will be able to write scripts that organize a
			sequence of computations into a clean, repeatable workflow, and write functions
			that accept inputs and return outputs — the foundation of modular programming in MATLAB.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<xi:include href="sec-scripts.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="sec-functions.ptx" />

	<conclusion>
		<title><e component="emoji">🗝️</e> Key Takeaways...</title>
		<assemblage xml:id="takeaways-scripts-functions">
			<p>
			<dl width="narrow">
				<li>
					<title>Scripts</title>
					<p>
						A script is a <pf>.m</pf> file containing a sequence of MATLAB commands that run
						top-to-bottom each time the script is executed.
						<ul marker="square">
							<li>Use <pf>clc; clear;</pf> at the top so every run starts from a clean state.</li>
							<li>Scripts share the base workspace — variables created in a script appear in the Workspace panel.</li>
							<li>Use <pf>fprintf</pf> to produce formatted, readable output.</li>
						</ul>
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<title>Functions</title>
					<p>
						A function is a reusable tool that accepts inputs and returns outputs.
						<ul marker="square">
							<li>The <term>function header</term> declares the name, inputs, and outputs: <pf>function out = name(in)</pf>.</li>
							<li>The file name must match the function name (e.g., <pf>coin_total.m</pf>).</li>
							<li>Functions use their own private workspace — internal variables do not appear in the Workspace after the function runs.</li>
							<li>To capture multiple outputs, use square brackets: <pf>[a, b] = f(x)</pf>.</li>
						</ul>
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<title>Scripts vs. Functions</title>
					<p>Both are <pf>.m</pf> files that execute commands top-to-bottom, but functions emphasize reusable input/output behavior and an isolated workspace.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<title>Functions Can Call Functions</title>
					<p>A function may call other functions, including ones you write yourself. This lets you build complex programs from simple, tested pieces.</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
		</p>
		</assemblage>
	</conclusion>

</chapter>
